"On graphs with large third eigenvalue (https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02563)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Title: On graphs with large third eigenvalue ... Given a graph $G$, let $\\lambda_3$ denote the third largest eigenvalue of its adjacency matrix. In this paper, we prove various results towards the conjecture that $\\lambda_3(G) \\le \\frac{|V(G)|}{3}$, motivated by a question of Nikiforov.\nTitle: On graphs with large third eigenvalue\nAuthors: Giacomo Leonida, Sida Li\nDate: Sun Jan  5 14:38:45 2025\n\nGiven a graph $G$, let $\\lambda_3$ denote the third largest eigenvalue of its adjacency matrix. In this paper, we prove various results towards the conjecture that $\\lambda_3(G) \\le \\frac{|V(G)|}{3}$, motivated by a question of Nikiforov. We generalise the known constructions that yield $\\lambda_3(G) = \\frac{|V(G)|}{3} - 1$ and prove the inequality holds for $G$ strongly regular, a regular line graph or a Cayley graph on an abelian group. We also consider the extended problem of minimising $\\lambda_{n-1}$ on weighted graphs and reduce the existence of a minimiser with simple final eigenvalue to a vertex multiplication of a graph on 11 vertices. We prove that the minimal $\\lambda_{n-1}$ over weighted graphs is at most $O(\\sqrt{n})$ from the minimal $\\lambda_{n-1}$ over unweighted graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectral gap of the normalized distance Laplacian (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10629)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; The smallest positive eigenvalue $\\partial_2$ of the normalized distance Laplacian matrix $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathcal{L}}$ of a connected graph is called its \\emph{spectral gap} and is intimately related to the Cheeger constant of $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathcal{L}}$. ... We prove the following stronger result: for any connected graph $G$ of order at least 2, \\[\\partial_2 \\ge \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{4}{3\\,t_{\\max}},\\] where $t_{\\max}$ denotes the maximum transmission in $G$.\nTitle: Spectral gap of the normalized distance Laplacian\nAuthors: Hitesh Kumar, Kamal Lochan Patra\nDate: Tue Aug 11 08:15:29 2026\n\nThe smallest positive eigenvalue $\\partial_2$ of the normalized distance Laplacian matrix $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathcal{L}}$ of a connected graph is called its \\emph{spectral gap} and is intimately related to the Cheeger constant of $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathcal{L}}$. Byrne, Johnston, Schildkraut and Tait (2025) conjectured that \\[ \\partial_2 \\ge \\frac{2}{3}\\] for all connected graphs. We prove the following stronger result: for any connected graph $G$ of order at least 2, \\[\\partial_2 \\ge \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{4}{3\\,t_{\\max}},\\] where $t_{\\max}$ denotes the maximum transmission in $G$. Moreover, equality holds if and only if $G\\cong K_{m,m}$ for some $m\\ge 1$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDegree conjecture relating interior and gamma polynomials of bipartite graphs — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/353399/degree-conjecture-relating-interior-and-gamma-polynomials-of)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Degree conjecture relating interior and gamma polynomials of bipartite graphs ... The degrees of $I_{\\mathcal{G}}$I G​ and $\\gamma_{\\mathcal{G}}$γ G​ agree.\n\nDegree conjecture relating interior and gamma polynomials of bipartite graphs\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet ${\\mathcal{G}}$G be a bipartite graph. Denote by $I_{\\mathcal{G}}$I G​ its interior polynomial and by $\\gamma_{\\mathcal{G}}$γ G​ its gamma-polynomial. Degree conjecture. The degrees of $I_{\\mathcal{G}}$I G​ and $\\gamma_{\\mathcal{G}}$γ G​ agree. This conjecture is based on computations for 12,000 randomly chosen bipartite graphs with 6 to 11 vertices; its general validity remains open.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Tamás Kálmán and Lilla Tóthmérész, “Ehrhart theory of symmetric edge polytopes via ribbon structures”, arXiv:2201.10501 (2022).\n\nbipartite-graphsgraph-polynomials\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn graphs with large third eigenvalue | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/ja/abs/2501.02563)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Given a graph $G$G, let $\\lambda_3$λ 3​ denote the third largest eigenvalue of its adjacency matrix. In this paper, we prove various results towards the conjecture that $\\lambda_3(G) \\le \\frac{|V(G)|}{3}$λ 3​(G)≤3∣V(G)∣​, motivated by a question of Nikiforov. ... @misc{leonida2026graphslargeeigenvalue, title={On graphs with large third eigenvalue}, author={Giacomo Leonida and Sida Li}, year={2026}, eprint={2501.02563}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02563}, }\nSubmitted 21 Jan 2025\n\n# On graphs with large third eigenvalue\n\nImageUniversity of Cambridge\n\nGL\n\nGiacomo Leonida\n\nSL\n\nSida Li\n\n## Abstract\n\nGiven a graph $G$G, let $\\lambda_3$λ 3​ denote the third largest eigenvalue of its adjacency matrix. In this paper, we prove various results towards the conjecture that $\\lambda_3(G) \\le \\frac{|V(G)|}{3}$λ 3​(G)≤3∣V(G)∣​, motivated by a question of Nikiforov. We generalise the known constructions that yield $\\lambda_3(G) = \\frac{|V(G)|}{3} - 1$λ 3​(G)=3∣V(G)∣​−1 and prove the inequality holds for $G$G strongly regular, a regular line graph or a Cayley graph on an abelian group. We also consider the extended problem of minimising $\\lambda_{n-1}$λ n−1​ on weighted graphs and reduce the existence of a minimiser with simple final eigenvalue to a vertex multiplication of a graph on 11 vertices. We prove that the minimal $\\lambda_{n-1}$λ n−1​ over weighted graphs is at most $O(\\sqrt{n})$O(n​) from the minimal $\\lambda_{n-1}$λ n−1​ over unweighted graphs.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{leonida2026graphslargeeigenvalue, title={On graphs with large third eigenvalue}, author={Giacomo Leonida and Sida Li}, year={2026}, eprint={2501.02563}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02563}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectral gap of the normalized distance Laplacian · Pith Review (https://pith.science/paper/2608.10629?design=next)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last month; Pith's one-line read The distance Laplacian spectral gap always exceeds 2/3\n\ndesk verdict A clean, self-contained proof that resolves the Byrne et al. conjecture on the spectral gap of the normalized distance Laplacian; the paper deserves a serious referee. read the letter →\n\n### The reading\n\nThis paper proves a sharp lower bound for the smallest positive eigenvalue, the spectral gap, of the normalized distance Laplacian of any connected graph. The bound is 2/3 + 4/(3 t_max), where t_max is the maximum vertex transmission, and it is strictly larger than the uniform constant 2/3 conjectured earlier. As a consequence, the uniform conjecture follows, and no finite graph actually attains 2/3; the bound is asymptotically approached by balanced complete bipartite graphs K_{m,m}. The paper also characterizes equality, showing that a graph meets the bound exactly when it is isomorphic to K_{m,m} for some m.\n\n### What carries the argument\n\nThe central mechanism is the inequality of Claim 2.1: any nonzero vector y with 1^T y = 0 satisfies y^T T y - 3 y^T D y ≥ 4||y||^2, where T is the transmission matrix and D the distance matrix. It is proved by writing y as the difference of its positive and negative parts and using the triangle inequality to show that 2p^T D q - p^T D p ≥ 2||p||^2 and similarly for q. This inequality, inserted into the min-max characterization of ∂2 with the bound y^T T y ≤ t_max ||y||^2, yields the sharp lower bound. The equality analysis then forces all transmissions to equal t_max and the positive and negative supports of the eigenvector to form the two parts of a balanced complete bipartite graph.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProve the third-eigenvalue proportion conjecture (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/prove-third-eigenvalue-proportion-one-third)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; — On graphs with large third eigenvalue  (2501.02563 - Leonida et al., 5 Jan 2025) in Section 6, Concluding remarks ... Bound the penultimate eigenvalue of vertex multiplications of T  Same Paper ... Regular-graph case of the Nurdin–Baskoro–Salman–Gaos conjecture  2609.30114\n\n# Prove the third-eigenvalue proportion conjecture\n\nProve that the supremum c₃ of λ₃(G)/|V(G)| over all finite simple graphs G equals 1/3.\n\n### Background\n\nThe paper proves λ₃(G) ≤ |V(G)|/3 for several substantial graph classes, including strongly regular graphs, regular line graphs, Cayley graphs on abelian groups, semi-Cayley graphs on abelian groups, and vertex-transitive graphs with abelian automorphism group. It also constructs families whose normalized third eigenvalue approaches 1/3.\n\nDespite this evidence, the universal inequality remains unresolved in the paper. The conjecture is the broader form of the motivating problem attributed to Nikiforov and is not settled by the partial class-specific results.\n\n### References\n\nWith $\\lambda_3 \\le \\frac{n}{3}$ proven for strong candidates for counter-examples, we also conjecture that $c_3 = \\frac13$.\n\n— On graphs with large third eigenvalue  (2501.02563 - Leonida et al., 5 Jan 2025) in Section 6, Concluding remarks\n\n### Related Problems\n\n1.\n\nClassify graphs attaining the known third-eigenvalue construction  Same Paper\n\n2.\n\nProve the weighted penultimate-eigenvalue conjecture  Same Paper\n\n3.\n\nBound the penultimate eigenvalue of vertex multiplications of T  Same Paper\n\n4.\n\nDerive explicit penultimate-root bounds for vertex multiplications of T  Same Paper\n\n5.\n\nRegular-graph case of the Nurdin–Baskoro–Salman–Gaos conjecture  2609.30114\n\n6.\n\nCharacterize graphs with exactly three normalized Laplacian eigenvalues  2402.09160\n\n7.\n\nConstant-factor approximation for sparsest cut on Abelian Cayley graphs  2609.05368 2609.05368\n\n8.\n\nWeaker bound allowing zero eigenvalues  2508.01163\n\n9.\n\nVerstraete’s 1/3-Conjecture for cubic graphs of girth at least 6  2401.17820\n\n10.\n\nGeneral inertia bound for graphs  2508.01163--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the normalized distance laplacian eigenvalues of graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0096300322006889)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Volume 438, 1 February 2023, 127615 ... The normalized distance Laplacian matrix (𝐷ℒ-matrix) of a connected graph Γ is defined by 𝐷ℒ⁡(Γ)=𝐼−𝑇⁢𝑟⁢(Γ)−1/2=𝐷⁡(Γ)⁢𝑇⁢𝑟⁢(Γ)−1/2; where 𝐷⁡(Γ) is the distance matrix and 𝑇⁢𝑟⁡(Γ) is the diagonal matrix of the vertex transmissions in Γ. ... Characterize the graphs having exactly two distinct 𝐷ℒ-eigenvalues which in turn solves a conjecture proposed in the literature.\n\n## Applied Mathematics and Computation\n\nVolume 438, 1 February 2023, 127615\n\n# On the normalized distance laplacian eigenvalues of graphs\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.amc.2022.127615Get rights and contentView accessibility information\n\n## Highlights\n\n  * •\n\nThe normalized distance Laplacian matrix (𝐷ℒ-matrix) of a connected graph Γ is defined by 𝐷ℒ⁡(Γ)=𝐼−𝑇⁢𝑟⁢(Γ)−1/2=𝐷⁡(Γ)⁢𝑇⁢𝑟⁢(Γ)−1/2; where 𝐷⁡(Γ) is the distance matrix and 𝑇⁢𝑟⁡(Γ) is the diagonal matrix of the vertex transmissions in Γ.\n\n  * •\n\nCharacterize the graphs having exactly two distinct 𝐷ℒ-eigenvalues which in turn solves a conjecture proposed in the literature.\n\n  * •\n\nDetermine the bounds for the 𝐷ℒ-spectral radius and the second smallest eigenvalue of 𝐷ℒ⁡(Γ)-matrix, and identify the candidate graphs attaining them.\n\n  * •\n\nIdentify the classes of graphs whose second smallest 𝐷ℒ-eigenvalue is 1 and relate it with the distance spectrum of such graphs.\n\n  * •\n\nIntroduce the concept of the trace norm (the normalized distance Laplacian energy 𝐷⁢ℒ⁢𝐸⁡(Γ) of Γ) of 𝐼−𝐷ℒ⁡(Γ).\n\n  * •\n\nObtain some bounds on 𝐷⁢ℒ⁢𝐸⁡(Γ) and characterize the corresponding extremal graphs.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe normalized distance Laplacian matrix (𝐷ℒ-matrix) of a connected graph Γ is defined by 𝐷ℒ⁡(Γ)=𝐼−𝑇⁢𝑟⁢(Γ)−1/2⁢𝐷⁡(Γ)⁢𝑇⁢𝑟⁢(Γ)−1/2, where 𝐷⁡(Γ) is the distance matrix and 𝑇⁢𝑟⁡(Γ) is the diagonal matrix of the vertex transmissions in Γ. In this article, we present interesting spectral properties of 𝐷ℒ⁡(Γ)-matrix. We characterize the graphs having exactly two distinct 𝐷ℒ-eigenvalues which in turn solves a conjecture proposed in [26]. We characterize the complete multipartite graphs with three distinct 𝐷ℒ-eigenvalues. We present the bounds for the 𝐷ℒ-spectral radius and the second smallest eigenvalue of 𝐷ℒ⁡(Γ)-matrix and identify the candidate graphs attaining them. We also identify the classes of graphs whose second smallest 𝐷ℒ-eigenvalue is 1 and relate it with the distance spectrum of such graphs. Further, we introduce the concept of the trace norm (the normalized distance Laplacian energy 𝐷⁢ℒ⁢𝐸⁡(Γ) of Γ) of 𝐼−𝐷ℒ⁡(Γ). We obtain some bounds and characterize the corresponding extremal graphs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n14th Emléktábla Workshop                                                                 13 (https://www.renyi.hu/~emlektab/emlektabla13.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Suppose now that \\(G\\) is bipartite. ... With Tamás Kálmán, we noticed that whenever we computed these two polynomials, their degrees a